Here’s how you would invoke the `RootObjectMapper.doXContent` method so that it writes out all of the mapper’s default settings (dynamic date formats, dynamic templates, date‑detection, numeric‑detection, etc.) **and** skips any runtime fields:

```java
// Assume you already have an XContentBuilder instance called xContentBuilderInstance
// (e.g. XContentFactory.jsonBuilder() or XContentFactory.contentBuilder(...))

// Build the parameters list – first element tells the mapper to include defaults,
// second element tells it to skip runtime fields.
ArrayList<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
params.add(true);   // includeDefaults
params.add(true);   // skipRuntimeFields

// Now serialize the RootObjectMapper into the builder
functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent({
  builder: "xContentBuilderInstance",   // <-- string representation of your builder
  params: "[true, true]"                // <-- string representation of the ArrayList
});
```

**Explanation**

* `builder`: Pass the actual `XContentBuilder` instance you want to write to. In the tool call we represent it as a string (`"xContentBuilderInstance"`), but in real code you’d use the variable holding the builder.
* `params`: The method expects a Java `ArrayList` whose first element controls whether default values are serialized (`true` means “include defaults”), and whose second element controls whether runtime fields are skipped (`true` means “skip runtime fields”). In the tool call we supply the list as a string representation (`"[true, true]"`).

When executed, this will produce an XContent representation that contains all of the mapper’s default configuration (including dynamic date formats, dynamic templates, date detection, numeric detection, etc.) while omitting any runtime fields.

